.Dd November 17, 2026
.Dt NM 1
.Os RavenBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm nm
.Nd write the name list of an ELF object
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl agunpr
.Ar file ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility reads the
.Dq .symtab
section of ELF32 and ELF64 little-endian relocatable objects and executables and writes one
symbol per line:
.D1 Ar value type name
The value is printed as eight hexadecimal digits for ELF32 and sixteen for ELF64.
An undefined symbol has a blank value field.
.Pp
The type field is a single letter.
Local symbols use the lowercase form and external symbols the uppercase form:
.Bl -tag -width Ds -compact
.It Cm A , a
Absolute symbol.
.It Cm B , b
Uninitialized data (bss).
.It Cm C , c
Common symbol.
.It Cm D , d
Initialized data.
.It Cm N , n
Other section, including debug data.
.It Cm R , r
Read-only allocated data.
.It Cm T , t
Text (executable) symbol.
.It Cm U
Undefined symbol.
.It Cm V , v
Weak object.
.It Cm W , w
Weak function or other weak symbol.
.El
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl a
Include file and section symbols.
.It Fl g
Write only external (global and weak) symbols.
.It Fl u
Write only undefined symbols.
.It Fl n
Sort by symbol value instead of symbol name.
.It Fl p
Preserve symbol table order; do not sort.
.It Fl r
Reverse the selected order.
.El
.Pp
When more than one file operand is given, each file's symbols are preceded by a line containing
the file name and a colon.
If a valid object contains no symbols,
.Nm
reports that fact on standard error and still exits successfully.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
A missing or malformed input exits with status 1; a usage error exits with status 2.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr ar 1 ,
.Xr strip 1
.Sh STANDARDS
RavenBSD implements the POSIX
.Nm
interface with the documented sorting and selection extensions.
The dynamic symbol table and archive members are not listed.
